{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791040865,"nonce":"yQGvGxALFZ5k+EAZ+HXl4oCyLJ0=","title":"A downlevel adapter cannot create a device, and nothing reports what it lacks","message":"requested_limits starts from wgpu::Limits::default() and keeps every limit at or above it (or_better_values_from), so a device request on an adapter below WebGPU's defaults (GLES, WebGL2: no storage buffers, 2048 texels, 15 inter-stage variables) asks for more than the adapter has and fails. hlwgpu's Android build includes GLES, so a GLES-only Android device cannot open one.\n\nThe defaults are now lowered to what the adapter has (or_worse_values_from) before requested limits raise them; a WebGPU adapter meets every default and is asked for the same as before.\n\nA framework choosing its shaders also needs to know what such an adapter lacks (Blinc checks VERTEX_STORAGE). GpuAdapter.supportsDownlevel(DownlevelFlag) answers from wgpu's DownlevelFlags, the enum generated from wgpu's own catalog as NativeFeature is. Browser WebGPU is a full implementation, so the web backend answers true.","files":null}]}